.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt ROFF 7
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm roff
.Nd roff language reference for manual pages
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
language is the input format underneath the
.Xr mdoc 7
and
.Xr man 7
macro languages.
This page describes the part of it that
.Xr oxdoc 1
implements: the requests and escape sequences that manual pages use.
Page layout, diversions, traps and environments are not implemented, as in
the other BSDs' formatter.
.Ss Input lines
An input line is either a text line or a control line.
A control line starts with the control character, normally
.Ql \&. ,
or the no-break control character
.Ql \(aq ,
followed by optional spaces and tabs, a request or macro name, and its
arguments.
Any other line is a text line.
.Pp
Arguments are separated by spaces.
An argument that contains spaces is enclosed in double quotes; inside
quotes, two double quotes stand for one.
.Pp
A line ending with an odd number of backslashes continues on the next input
line.
A control line consisting of the control character alone is ignored.
.Ss Comments
.Ql \e\(dq
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.
A line consisting of the control character and a comment is ignored
entirely.
.Ql \e#
is a comment that also removes the newline.
.Sh REQUESTS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic \&ds Ar name Op Ar string
Define a string.
A leading double quote in
.Ar string
is removed, so that it can start with spaces.
.It Ic \&as Ar name Op Ar string
Append to a string.
.It Ic \&nr Ar name Oo \(pl|\- Oc Ns Ar number
Set, increase or decrease a number register.
.It Ic \&rr Ar name ...
Remove number registers.
.It Ic \&rm Ar name ...
Remove strings or macros.
.It Ic \&rn Ar old new
Rename a string or macro.
.It Ic \&als Ar new old
Make
.Ar new
another name for the macro
.Ar old .
.It Ic \&de Ar name Op Ar end
Define a macro, up to a line holding the control character and
.Ar end ,
by default
.Ql \&.. .
In the definition,
.Ql \e\e
stands for one backslash, so that
.Ql \e\e$1
becomes the first argument when the macro is called.
.It Ic \&am Ar name Op Ar end
Append to a macro.
.It Ic \&ig Op Ar end
Ignore input up to a line holding the control character and
.Ar end .
.It Ic \&if Ar condition body
Process
.Ar body
if
.Ar condition
holds.
.It Ic \&ie Ar condition body
The same, remembering the result for the next
.Ic \&el .
.It Ic \&el Ar body
Process
.Ar body
if the last
.Ic \&ie
condition did not hold.
.It Ic \&so Ar file
Include
.Ar file ,
relative to the root of the manual tree.
.It Ic \&tr Ar pairs
Translate each first character of a pair to the second.
.It Ic \&cc Ar c
Use
.Ar c
as the control character.
.It Ic \&ec Op Ar c
Use
.Ar c
as the escape character, by default a backslash.
.It Ic \&eo
Turn escape sequences off.
.It Ic \&nop Ar text
Treat
.Ar text
as a text line.
.El
.Pp
A
.Ar body
is the rest of the line, or, starting with
.Ql \e{ ,
all lines up to the matching
.Ql \e} .
.Pp
A
.Ar condition
is one of:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm n , o
true (terminal output, odd page);
.It Cm t , e , v
false;
.It Cm d Ar name
a string or macro
.Ar name
is defined;
.It Cm r Ar name
a number register
.Ar name
is defined;
.It Cm c Ar char
true;
.It Ar \(aqa\(aqb\(aq
the strings
.Ar a
and
.Ar b ,
after interpolation, are equal;
.It Ar expression
a numeric expression is greater than zero.
.El
Any condition may be negated with
.Ql \&! .
Numeric expressions use
.Ql \(pl ,
.Ql \- ,
.Ql * ,
.Ql / ,
.Ql % ,
.Ql < ,
.Ql > ,
.Ql <= ,
.Ql >= ,
.Ql = ,
.Ql == ,
.Ql <> ,
.Ql &
and
.Ql \&:
evaluated from left to right, and parentheses.
.Pp
Requests that only affect typesetting \(em hyphenation, adjustment, page
size, fonts and the like \(em are accepted and have no effect.
.Sh ESCAPE SEQUENCES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic \e\e , \ee
A backslash.
.It Ic \e\-
A minus sign.
.It Ic \e&
A zero-width character.
It stops a word from being taken as a macro name or a delimiter, and a
period before it from ending a sentence.
.It Ic \e\  , \e~ , \e0
A space that does not break the line.
.It Ic \e| , \e^ , \e% , \e:
Nothing on a terminal.
.It Ic \ec
At the end of a text line, join the next line without a space.
.It Ic \e(xx , \e[name] , \eC\(aqname\(aq
The special character
.Ar name ,
such as
.Ql \e(em
for an em dash; see
.Xr mandoc_char 7
on other systems for the list.
.Ic \e[u Ns Ar XXXX Ns Ic \&]
is the Unicode character U+XXXX.
.It Ic \eN\(aq Ns Ar number Ns Ic \(aq
The character with code
.Ar number .
.It Ic \ef Ns Ar font
Change the font:
.Cm R
(roman),
.Cm B
(bold),
.Cm I
(italic, shown underlined),
.Cm BI ,
.Cm CW ,
or
.Cm P ,
the previous font.
Names longer than one character are written
.Ic \ef( Ns Ar xx
or
.Ic \ef[ Ns Ar name Ns Ic \&] .
.It Ic \e* Ns Ar c , \e*( Ns Ar xx , \e*[ Ns Ar name Ns Ic \&]
Interpolate a string.
.It Ic \en Ns Ar c , \en( Ns Ar xx , \en[ Ns Ar name Ns Ic \&]
Interpolate a number register;
.Ic \en+ Ns Ar c
and
.Ic \en\- Ns Ar c
increment or decrement it first.
.It Ic \e$ Ns Ar n , \e$* , \e$@
Inside a macro: its
.Ar n Ns th
argument, or all of them, the second form quoted.
.It Ic \es Ns Ar size , \em Ns Ar color , \eh\(aq Ns Ar width Ns Ic \(aq
Size, color and motion; on a terminal, horizontal motion prints spaces and
the others nothing.
.It Ic \ew\(aq Ns Ar text Ns Ic \(aq
The width of
.Ar text .
.El
.Pp
An escape sequence not listed prints its character.
